 2. Warsh&#x27;s telling silence 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Federal Reserve chairman Kevin Warsh speaks with IMF managing director Kristalina Georgieva in Bangkok on Friday. Photo: Matt Hunt/Anadolu via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Warsh had the opportunity late last night to signal that another interest rate hike is imminent. He did not take it.

 Why it matters: Warsh did nothing to push back against expectations that policymakers will leave interest rates unchanged at their Oct. 27-28 meeting, even as data this week showed inflation accelerating more quickly than expected.

 Financial markets took the silence as the signal. Odds of an October rate hike fell to 17% from 28% a day earlier, according to CME FedWatch — reversing the sharp rise after Wednesday's hot inflation data. What they're saying: Speaking at the IMF's annual meetings in Bangkok, Warsh said economic developments had largely unfolded as expected since September's rate hike.

 "Over the month or so that's transpired since then, the world has broadly conformed to our expectations," said Warsh, who was interviewed by IMF managing director Kristalina Georgieva. "I don't prejudge meetings. I don't believe in saying now what I might do at the meetings that we have in the balance of this year," Warsh said. The big picture: His remarks follow signals from top Fed officials that they are in no rush to raise rates again, though another hike in December remains on the table.

 His appearance was the Fed's last public discussion of monetary policy before its pre-meeting blackout period that begins tomorrow. The intrigue: Warsh was optimistic about the investment surge fueled by the AI build-out, calling it "probably the most consequential change" to the U.S. and global economies in his lifetime.

 Of note: Warsh appears to have gotten the White House memo on the AI rebrand .

 "You pronounce it AI — in the U.S., we now pronounce it SI, just so you know," he joked, adopting President Trump's preferred term, "super intelligence." Go deeper 

 
 
